And then we would need to cast
somebody to play that female.

00:32:06.609 --> 00:32:09.187
And then Jenna suggested Paige.

00:32:09.187 --> 00:32:11.573
Her, you know, who she, who she knew from,

00:32:11.573 --> 00:32:13.285
what? Theater? From college.

00:32:13.285 --> 00:32:14.368
Kentucky theater.

00:32:14.368 --> 00:32:16.013
Uh, uh, right after college, yeah,

00:32:16.013 --> 00:32:17.521
Uh, Kentucky Shakespeare.

00:32:17.521 --> 00:32:19.242
Kentucky Shakespeare, yeah.

00:32:19.242 --> 00:32:21.491
So, you, Jenna was recommending Paige,

00:32:21.491 --> 00:32:24.803
because Paige is in a
band. Paige is a vocalist.

00:32:24.803 --> 00:32:28.144
And we had worked with Paige
before we even had met,

00:32:28.144 --> 00:32:32.811
and started working with
Jenna, which is so strange, so.

00:32:33.789 --> 00:32:35.633
We brought Paige in, she recorded the part

00:32:35.633 --> 00:32:37.649
for Hazel, nailed it.

00:32:37.649 --> 00:32:41.947
And she's also an actor and
can come in and play the part.

00:32:41.947 --> 00:32:43.177
So it was like a win-win.

00:32:43.177 --> 00:32:45.882
And like, she was already
in the mythical world

00:32:45.882 --> 00:32:48.699
because what we had
worked with her on before

00:32:48.699 --> 00:32:50.949
is Buddy System season one,

00:32:53.667 --> 00:32:56.119
when, let's see, there's the commercial

00:32:56.119 --> 00:32:57.759
for the Drain Weaver.

00:32:57.759 --> 00:32:59.425
Yeah.

00:32:59.425 --> 00:33:03.311
Which is, you know,
our co ex-girlfriend's,

00:33:03.311 --> 00:33:05.699
who's now became the media mogul

00:33:05.699 --> 00:33:08.172
with Billy May's type products,

00:33:08.172 --> 00:33:11.214
including all the hair that
goes into your shower drain.

00:33:11.214 --> 00:33:14.876
If you put the right
mold down in, in there,

00:33:14.876 --> 00:33:17.724
you can then, after,
an X number of showers,

00:33:17.724 --> 00:33:18.714
you can pull that out.

00:33:18.714 --> 00:33:20.027
And it will have an article of clothing

00:33:20.027 --> 00:33:24.597
that has been magically
woven from your body hair,

00:33:24.597 --> 00:33:26.336
and your head hair. All of your hairs.

00:33:26.336 --> 00:33:28.615
And we needed a model for the commercial.

00:33:28.615 --> 00:33:31.265
And the, the audition process was one

00:33:31.265 --> 00:33:33.744
of the more awkward audition processes

00:33:33.744 --> 00:33:36.076
that I've ever been a
part of, because it was

00:33:36.076 --> 00:33:39.696
bringing in women into our office.

00:33:39.696 --> 00:33:40.529
Now.

00:33:40.529 --> 00:33:44.362
Where me, you, Stevie,
the casting director.

00:33:45.676 --> 00:33:46.729
And it wasn't just the two of us.

00:33:46.729 --> 00:33:47.562
And probably a couple of other people.

00:33:47.562 --> 00:33:48.395
We were all sitting in our office.

00:33:48.395 --> 00:33:49.446
But still.

00:33:49.446 --> 00:33:52.145
When you bring a bunch of women,

00:33:52.145 --> 00:33:54.391
one at a time into your office,

00:33:54.391 --> 00:33:56.651
and the thing that they're
doing for their audition

00:33:56.651 --> 00:33:59.401
is acting like they're showering.

00:34:00.854 --> 00:34:02.050
And they don't have any lines...

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:03.133
That is, uh...

00:34:03.133 --> 00:34:04.058
It gets...

00:34:04.058 --> 00:34:06.237
We just, we did not anticipate...

00:34:06.237 --> 00:34:07.070
How weird it was gonna feel.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:08.450
How awkward that could get.

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:11.411
To watch women in our
office pantomime showering.

00:34:11.411 --> 00:34:12.474
And then just kind of sit there

00:34:12.474 --> 00:34:13.883
and like nod with a note pad.

00:34:13.883 --> 00:34:16.389
Like, oh yeah, she touches herself in a...

00:34:16.389 --> 00:34:17.222
What?

00:34:17.222 --> 00:34:19.115
I mean, it's like, what
do you do when you shower?

00:34:19.115 --> 00:34:21.512
You like, you're like touching,
you're, you're bathing

00:34:21.512 --> 00:34:22.533
and you're like reacting to the...

00:34:22.533 --> 00:34:23.366
Say, "Scrub yourself."

00:34:23.366 --> 00:34:24.862
Okay. Well, I mean, but
it wasn't, they were,

00:34:24.862 --> 00:34:26.283
they were touching
themselves, not in that way.

00:34:26.283 --> 00:34:27.313
Scrubbing themselves.

00:34:27.313 --> 00:34:28.146
Like in a shower way.

00:34:28.146 --> 00:34:29.345
Bathing themselves.

00:34:29.345 --> 00:34:31.510
Anyway, Paige did a great job at that.

00:34:32.372 --> 00:34:33.763
Hey, Paige seemed to understand...

00:34:33.763 --> 00:34:34.939
She understood the assignment.

00:34:34.939 --> 00:34:37.844
Because there was a voice over, like,

00:34:37.844 --> 00:34:40.481
"Have you ever thought about
all the hair that you send down

00:34:40.481 --> 00:34:43.290
the drain and never make into clothes?"

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:44.364
And then you're like, "Yes."

00:34:44.364 --> 00:34:46.892
You know, so there's,
obviously it was very comedic.

00:34:46.892 --> 00:34:48.418
So you've got to like, respond
to this voice over artist.

00:34:48.418 --> 00:34:49.543
Yeah. She was doing, she was doing the...

00:34:49.543 --> 00:34:50.637
She got it.

00:34:50.637 --> 00:34:51.493
I'm in an infomercial.

00:34:51.493 --> 00:34:54.109
I will say it's been a
long time. So, I mean...

00:34:54.109 --> 00:34:54.942
Let's tell this story.

00:34:54.942 --> 00:34:55.775
I don't think there's any way

00:34:55.775 --> 00:34:57.717
that anyone's going to be
able to trace this back

00:34:57.717 --> 00:34:58.876
to this particular person,

00:34:58.876 --> 00:35:00.966
because there was no record
of who auditioned that day.

00:35:00.966 --> 00:35:05.659
But there was someone who did
not understand the assignment.

00:35:05.659 --> 00:35:07.235
Uh, in two ways,

00:35:07.235 --> 00:35:08.068
Yeah.

00:35:08.068 --> 00:35:09.957
The first way that they did not understand

00:35:09.957 --> 00:35:11.999
the assignment is that
this particular woman

00:35:11.999 --> 00:35:13.628
thought that she was auditioning

00:35:13.628 --> 00:35:14.916
for the role of Amy Brielle's,

00:35:14.916 --> 00:35:18.021
which had already been given to Leslie.

00:35:18.021 --> 00:35:20.908
Talking about the lead
role in the entire series.

00:35:20.908 --> 00:35:21.741
To Leslie Bibb.

00:35:21.741 --> 00:35:24.702
And the way we found out was
because when she came in,

00:35:24.702 --> 00:35:27.426
she started showering.
She did start shower.

00:35:27.426 --> 00:35:28.259
She did.

00:35:28.259 --> 00:35:30.001
I don't know how she put
these two things together.

00:35:30.001 --> 00:35:31.879
I don't know what script she got ahold of,

00:35:31.879 --> 00:35:35.882
but then she, she launches into
a monologue while showering.

00:35:35.882 --> 00:35:37.046
Which of course, no one has done,

00:35:37.046 --> 00:35:38.877
because they don't have any lines.

00:35:38.877 --> 00:35:41.168
And then we realize, she is memorized...

00:35:41.168 --> 00:35:42.139
Leslie's line.

00:35:42.139 --> 00:35:44.852
And Amy Brielle's monologue.

00:35:44.852 --> 00:35:46.287
And we, well, again, we're nice guys.

00:35:46.287 --> 00:35:48.706
We're good Southern
boys. So we let her go.

00:35:48.706 --> 00:35:49.828
Through the whole thing.

00:35:49.828 --> 00:35:51.276
We were like, well, she's going to do it.

00:35:51.276 --> 00:35:52.123
Let's just see how well
she's memorized it.

00:35:52.123 --> 00:35:53.527
She's prepared.

00:35:53.527 --> 00:35:55.077
Maybe we'll recast the lead roll.

00:35:55.077 --> 00:35:56.696
I don't know who spoke first to say,

00:35:56.696 --> 00:35:58.467
"Ah, great job on that.

00:35:58.467 --> 00:36:01.064
That's, um, that was really good.

00:36:01.064 --> 00:36:05.085
But actually, this role
is just the shower girl."

00:36:05.977 --> 00:36:06.951
So can you do that all again?

00:36:06.951 --> 00:36:09.182
Without all the stuff you memorized?

00:36:09.182 --> 00:36:12.220
It felt, I felt so bad for
her. And no one else did it.

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:13.630
I mean, I don't think it was on us.

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:18.380
I have to also say that her
wardrobe choice that day...

00:36:19.308 --> 00:36:21.222
Well, I'll call it catsuit.

00:36:21.222 --> 00:36:24.904
It was a, it, it was
what you might expect.

00:36:24.904 --> 00:36:26.709
If you, if you thought like, oh,

00:36:26.709 --> 00:36:30.918
this is a shower scene in
like a different genre.

00:36:30.918 --> 00:36:32.010
Maybe, you know.

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:34.600
Yeah, like. If you were going
to be nude in the shower

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:35.945
and you wanted to...

00:36:35.945 --> 00:36:37.875
But you didn't want, you
didn't want your audition

00:36:37.875 --> 00:36:39.649
to be nude, but you wanted it to be like,

00:36:39.649 --> 00:36:42.198
but, just so you will know what
I look like without clothes

00:36:42.198 --> 00:36:45.094
on, I'm wearing clothes
that will allow you to come

00:36:45.094 --> 00:36:46.204
to those conclusions.

00:36:46.204 --> 00:36:48.289
There's a suit for that.
And she had it all.

00:36:48.289 --> 00:36:49.665
There, there, there is
an article of clothing

00:36:49.665 --> 00:36:50.498
and she had that on.

00:36:50.498 --> 00:36:53.952
It was just very tight
in every single place.

00:36:53.952 --> 00:36:56.526
And it gave the, I guess it's what, uh...

00:36:56.526 --> 00:36:57.470
We did learn a lot.

00:36:57.470 --> 00:37:01.008
You would wear if you
were going to be naked.

00:37:01.008 --> 00:37:01.841
I don't know.

00:37:01.841 --> 00:37:04.068
And the more I think about
it, it was a strategic choice.

00:37:04.068 --> 00:37:05.847
Not knowing where, where it could go.

00:37:05.847 --> 00:37:06.680
Yep, that's true.

00:37:06.680 --> 00:37:08.824
She didn't get the part. Paige, did.

00:37:08.824 --> 00:37:10.907
Long story. Paige got the part.

00:37:10.907 --> 00:37:14.168
It's wild that, like, Jenna knew Paige

00:37:14.168 --> 00:37:16.244
from way back when, then we met Paige,

00:37:16.244 --> 00:37:18.802
then we met Jenna and then
Jenna reminded us of Paige.

00:37:18.802 --> 00:37:21.852
And then Paige comes in
and like had a great time.

00:37:21.852 --> 00:37:22.685
Yeah.

00:37:22.685 --> 00:37:25.961
Again, it was weird
because like, it's Paige's,

00:37:25.961 --> 00:37:29.961
vocals as Hazel, but then
we never see her face.

00:37:31.425 --> 00:37:33.220
It's like, we're giving
her this limitation.

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:34.950
Like whenever we cast you
in something for either,

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:39.374
you're not going to talk at
all, or you're going to sing,

00:37:39.374 --> 00:37:42.278
but we're never going to see
your face, but it is Paige.

00:37:42.278 --> 00:37:43.111
It is.

00:37:43.111 --> 00:37:44.122
It's the back of her head.

00:37:44.122 --> 00:37:46.693
And she did an excellent
job. And if you know.

00:37:46.693 --> 00:37:48.039
One day we might put it together.

00:37:48.039 --> 00:37:49.247
She might have lines and a face.

00:37:49.247 --> 00:37:50.673
We're not making choices

00:37:50.673 --> 00:37:52.448
about not giving her lines or give,

00:37:52.448 --> 00:37:54.859
giving her face time because
of anything about her.

00:37:54.859 --> 00:37:57.147
It's just, she happens to
be involved in the projects

00:37:57.147 --> 00:37:58.528
where that happens to be the case.

00:37:58.528 --> 00:38:00.932
One interesting thing
about that and about,

00:38:00.932 --> 00:38:02.119
about Paige is that,

00:38:02.119 --> 00:38:05.453
of course, she was the focal point

00:38:05.453 --> 00:38:08.912
of all, of a lot of the
horror, you know, you,

00:38:08.912 --> 00:38:13.050
and we knew that we had this
idea about the backmasking

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:14.159
of playing it back,

00:38:14.159 --> 00:38:16.202
playing the record backwards
is the way that sort

00:38:16.202 --> 00:38:17.360
of the portal opens up.

00:38:17.360 --> 00:38:20.197
And that plays into these
sort of childhood fears.

00:38:20.197 --> 00:38:22.421
And, and some of the stuff that we said,

00:38:22.421 --> 00:38:24.737
I don't actually remember
a guy coming to our church

00:38:24.737 --> 00:38:25.570
to talk about it.

00:38:25.570 --> 00:38:27.178
But I think there was maybe
something that Campbell

00:38:27.178 --> 00:38:28.011
at one point.

00:38:28.011 --> 00:38:28.844
He came to Campbell, yeah.

00:38:28.844 --> 00:38:30.270
And he talked about backmasking
and of course we believed

00:38:30.270 --> 00:38:32.031
everything that anybody said
to us when we were kids,

00:38:32.031 --> 00:38:33.572
especially in a Christian context.

00:38:33.572 --> 00:38:34.772
So we were like, yeah, I guess the devil

00:38:34.772 --> 00:38:37.890
is influencing Led Zeppelin to send

00:38:37.890 --> 00:38:39.457
messages through the records backwards.

00:38:39.457 --> 00:38:41.701
It was a seminar that
I think he'd been doing

00:38:41.701 --> 00:38:44.227
for over a decade by that point.

00:38:44.227 --> 00:38:45.060
Yeah.

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:48.662
And it's like, but we were
still, like, mesmerized.

00:38:48.662 --> 00:38:51.826
So in that tends to be, and
first of all, backwards,

00:38:51.826 --> 00:38:55.141
singing and talking is unsettling

00:38:55.141 --> 00:38:57.758
for deep biological reasons, I guess.

00:38:57.758 --> 00:38:59.626
And it's, so, it's, it
is a very scary thing.

00:38:59.626 --> 00:39:01.379
So, we were like, do you
take this backwards...

00:39:01.379 --> 00:39:02.771
Deep biological reasons?

00:39:02.771 --> 00:39:03.604
I'm just saying that there's,

00:39:03.604 --> 00:39:05.672
you're intrinsically scared
of backwards talking.

00:39:05.672 --> 00:39:08.462
It's just weird. And
you know, you, you, you,

00:39:08.462 --> 00:39:11.759
you put it in the context
of this dark house

00:39:11.759 --> 00:39:13.341
with this woman singing.

00:39:13.341 --> 00:39:14.710
And then we knew, oh...

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:15.668
It's a threat.

00:39:15.668 --> 00:39:19.623
She's singing backwards. Let's
have her walking backwards.

00:39:19.623 --> 00:39:21.187
And then, I think this is a good time

00:39:21.187 --> 00:39:24.471
to talk about Ben's involvement in it.

00:39:24.471 --> 00:39:26.473
So, Ben Eck, who works for us has worked

00:39:26.473 --> 00:39:27.557
for us for a very long time.

00:39:27.557 --> 00:39:29.905
The only person that has
worked here longer is Stevie.

00:39:29.905 --> 00:39:32.105
Cause this is the first
person that Stevie helped

00:39:32.105 --> 00:39:33.379
us hire back for The Mythical Show.

00:39:33.379 --> 00:39:36.877
But Ben only worked for us
like a week less than Steve.

00:39:36.877 --> 00:39:37.710
Yeah, yeah.

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:38.543
Cause.

00:39:38.543 --> 00:39:39.376
Very, very soon after.

00:39:39.376 --> 00:39:40.380
Stevie and Ben had worked at together

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:41.520
at, at a previous job.

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:44.432
So then, she was like, this
is a guy I'm bringing in.

00:39:44.432 --> 00:39:45.265
Yeah.

00:39:45.265 --> 00:39:48.703
And, um, yeah, he's been, I mean, he's,

00:39:48.703 --> 00:39:51.977
he's instrumental on the
Mythical Kitchen side now,

00:39:51.977 --> 00:39:54.549
you'll hear Josh and
the team talking to Ben.

00:39:54.549 --> 00:39:56.007
Yeah.

00:39:56.007 --> 00:39:58.242
But when we have ideas
like this, because Ben,

00:39:58.242 --> 00:40:02.592
Ben was there for all those
classic sketches, music videos,

00:40:02.592 --> 00:40:04.414
he was there for buddy system.

00:40:04.414 --> 00:40:08.053
He understands what we're
trying to get across creatively

00:40:08.053 --> 00:40:09.678
and narratively, which again,

00:40:09.678 --> 00:40:12.650
we don't do a whole lot of that
except writing and pitching

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:14.847
at this point. So the making
of the narrative stuff,

00:40:14.847 --> 00:40:15.680
it was like, all right, well,

00:40:15.680 --> 00:40:17.982
let's bring Ben back into this process

00:40:17.982 --> 00:40:19.961
and get him really
involved. He's going to,

00:40:19.961 --> 00:40:20.794
he's going to be essentially
directing this, uh,

00:40:20.794 --> 00:40:24.178
cause we're going to be
in it the whole time. So.

00:40:24.178 --> 00:40:25.011
And he was essential.

00:40:25.011 --> 00:40:25.844
Yeah.

00:40:25.844 --> 00:40:26.700
As the director.

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:28.358
And then I think the thing about Ben,

00:40:28.358 --> 00:40:33.358
because he does have a lot of
filmmaking experience is that,

00:40:33.989 --> 00:40:37.843
you know, when he took a look
at the script, it was like,

00:40:37.843 --> 00:40:41.406
"Hey, here's my ideas for
the places that we could up

00:40:41.406 --> 00:40:43.561
the, the, you know,
this is my idea for how

00:40:43.561 --> 00:40:46.623
we could capture this
to make it even scarier.

00:40:46.623 --> 00:40:47.897
This is, you know."

00:40:47.897 --> 00:40:48.730
Were there even crow's hitting

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:51.110
the window before he got ahold of it?

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:51.943
Yeah.

00:40:51.943 --> 00:40:54.027
There was, but there was a lot of,

00:40:54.027 --> 00:40:56.942
he had a lot of thought on how to do that

00:40:56.942 --> 00:40:57.977
and how to achieve it.

00:40:57.977 --> 00:41:00.477
Ben's, Ben's, biggest, uh...

00:41:02.183 --> 00:41:03.359
Thing that he brings to the table

00:41:03.359 --> 00:41:06.432
that we don't typically
think about is the,

00:41:06.432 --> 00:41:08.765
until we get into it is the,

00:41:10.174 --> 00:41:13.872
you've got this idea
and you've written it.

00:41:13.872 --> 00:41:16.288
How do you capture it visually in a way

00:41:16.288 --> 00:41:18.813
that will communicate
what you actually want

00:41:18.813 --> 00:41:20.246
to communicate to your audience?

00:41:20.246 --> 00:41:23.597
And especially in the
horror genre, which again...

00:41:23.597 --> 00:41:24.430
Right.

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:25.678
I've, I've watched a lot of horror movies

00:41:25.678 --> 00:41:29.446
and have a sense of what
make of the types of themes,

00:41:29.446 --> 00:41:33.969
and visuals, and ideas
that make people scared,

00:41:33.969 --> 00:41:35.768
but translating that into,

00:41:35.768 --> 00:41:38.485
and this is how, so in other words,

00:41:38.485 --> 00:41:40.762
so one of the ideas we had was we want her

00:41:40.762 --> 00:41:42.326
to always be walking backwards,

00:41:42.326 --> 00:41:46.411
but Ben was able to
literally do a test shoot

00:41:46.411 --> 00:41:49.047
where he's like the way
to make this super creepy

00:41:49.047 --> 00:41:52.012
is to put her dress on backwards

00:41:52.012 --> 00:41:54.249
and to have her long stringy hair,

00:41:54.249 --> 00:41:57.264
which is not her hair, it's
a wig, in front of her face.

00:41:57.264 --> 00:41:59.707
So she looks like she's
facing away, but her arms are,

00:41:59.707 --> 00:42:01.235
there's a little, it's a little off.

00:42:01.235 --> 00:42:04.847
And then when she moves
towards you it's even freakier

00:42:04.847 --> 00:42:07.967
because her legs are moving
in a way as if she was walking

00:42:07.967 --> 00:42:10.622
normally, but she's facing backwards.

00:42:10.622 --> 00:42:12.628
Whereas if you try the thing
we were first thinking about,

00:42:12.628 --> 00:42:13.662
It's unsettling.

00:42:13.662 --> 00:42:14.495
Which is like,

00:42:14.495 --> 00:42:15.585
let's just have her
literally walking backwards,

00:42:15.585 --> 00:42:16.635
it kind of just looks like

00:42:16.635 --> 00:42:19.132
a normal person walking backwards.

00:42:19.132 --> 00:42:21.080
And all of a sudden
you're taken out of it.

00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:24.278
Horror is difficult to do. As is comedy.

00:42:24.278 --> 00:42:27.885
That's why there should
be more Oscar categories

00:42:27.885 --> 00:42:29.062
for each of those,

00:42:29.062 --> 00:42:33.681
because it is a challenging
art form to elicit

00:42:33.681 --> 00:42:35.492
those type of responses.

00:42:35.492 --> 00:42:38.683
You know? It's a lot
easier to make somebody cry

00:42:38.683 --> 00:42:41.088
than it is to scare the
shit out of somebody

00:42:41.088 --> 00:42:43.609
or to make them pee their pants laughing.

00:42:43.609 --> 00:42:44.776
In my opinion,

00:42:46.274 --> 00:42:48.612
speaking, generally, not specifically.

00:42:48.612 --> 00:42:50.995
So Ben had to translate the script

00:42:50.995 --> 00:42:52.435
in a way that would, okay.

00:42:52.435 --> 00:42:54.797
This is how, these are the moments

00:42:54.797 --> 00:42:56.905
where you've got to get
the scares and here's,

00:42:56.905 --> 00:42:58.888
here's how we're going to go for that.

00:42:58.888 --> 00:43:01.971
There's an added challenge of working

00:43:03.280 --> 00:43:08.106
within the rules of TikTok
and it being one phone.

00:43:08.106 --> 00:43:09.520
Yeah, literally shooting with a phone.

00:43:09.520 --> 00:43:11.920
We could have taught ourselves
into each having a phone,

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:15.921
but that's not really how
we would conduct like the,

00:43:15.921 --> 00:43:19.077
you know, writing a song
all night on, on, on TikTok.

00:43:19.077 --> 00:43:20.490
The thing that we said we were doing,

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:23.740
we had to keep the conceit true, right?

00:43:24.623 --> 00:43:27.377
So we had one phone filming vertically,

00:43:27.377 --> 00:43:28.815
and then there's handoffs.

00:43:28.815 --> 00:43:32.237
And there's, there's places
where you would stop filming.

00:43:32.237 --> 00:43:34.470
And there's places where
you'd keep filming.

00:43:34.470 --> 00:43:37.083
There's places where you'd
miraculously keep filming

00:43:37.083 --> 00:43:38.596
while you were freaking out. It's like,

00:43:38.596 --> 00:43:40.290
you got to take all
these things into account

00:43:40.290 --> 00:43:42.370
to make it as real as possible.

00:43:42.370 --> 00:43:45.953
Getting into those
details was so much fun.

00:43:47.054 --> 00:43:49.708
As with Derek talking about the way

00:43:49.708 --> 00:43:51.924
that he's going to export the songs so

00:43:51.924 --> 00:43:55.153
that we can play them in
the room versus having

00:43:55.153 --> 00:43:57.650
to add them in post.

00:43:57.650 --> 00:44:01.274
So that there's proximity when you're,

00:44:01.274 --> 00:44:03.168
when you're moving the phone around.

00:44:03.168 --> 00:44:06.688
So if you listen with
headphones on, if you, you know,

00:44:06.688 --> 00:44:09.113
that's a great way to rewatch Hazel

00:44:09.113 --> 00:44:11.585
because of the amount of sound design,

00:44:11.585 --> 00:44:15.264
because proximity and sound location is a,

00:44:15.264 --> 00:44:17.014
is a big part of the story.

00:44:17.014 --> 00:44:17.847
Yeah.

00:44:17.847 --> 00:44:20.311
As is things like, um,

00:44:20.311 --> 00:44:21.792
and you knew this when you wrote

00:44:21.792 --> 00:44:23.555
it in the second scene, is that we've got

00:44:23.555 --> 00:44:26.555
to establish the layout of the house

00:44:27.396 --> 00:44:30.778
within the context of us
writing a song on TikTok

00:44:30.778 --> 00:44:33.861
so that later, the viewer understands

00:44:35.348 --> 00:44:37.023
where Hazel is coming from

00:44:37.023 --> 00:44:41.054
and where she's taking me and taking you,

00:44:41.054 --> 00:44:43.870
you have to understand
spatially what's going on...

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:44.703
Mhm.

00:44:44.703 --> 00:44:47.827
So that you can, to set
you up to be scared.

00:44:47.827 --> 00:44:49.465
So, so that you have expectations

00:44:49.465 --> 00:44:51.717
when you're walking towards this door,

00:44:51.717 --> 00:44:53.117
you should be the most scared.

00:44:53.117 --> 00:44:53.950
Yeah.

00:44:53.950 --> 00:44:54.783
As an example,

00:44:54.783 --> 00:44:58.221
there's a lot of logistics and
details that go into getting

00:44:58.221 --> 00:45:00.465
a certain response from an audience.

00:45:00.465 --> 00:45:01.895
And again, that's what I can't wait

00:45:01.895 --> 00:45:04.725
to see because in between clips, people,

00:45:04.725 --> 00:45:08.345
hopefully, people will be
talking about it and making sense

00:45:08.345 --> 00:45:11.342
of what's happening and
waiting for the next,

00:45:11.342 --> 00:45:13.027
the next clip minutes, or...

00:45:13.027 --> 00:45:13.860
Well, and, and...

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:14.693
10 minutes later.

00:45:14.693 --> 00:45:15.777
My hope is, again,

00:45:15.777 --> 00:45:20.102
it's very difficult to
be genuinely frightened

00:45:20.102 --> 00:45:22.790
by something that you wrote.

00:45:22.790 --> 00:45:23.623
Yeah.

00:45:23.623 --> 00:45:27.268
Now, so my process is always, okay.

00:45:27.268 --> 00:45:29.351
I, I don't like to see...

00:45:30.742 --> 00:45:32.996
The first couple of
passes of the edit, right?

00:45:32.996 --> 00:45:36.645
Like you, you, you were
working with Ben on that.

00:45:36.645 --> 00:45:38.376
And I like to see it
once it's kind of like,

00:45:38.376 --> 00:45:41.841
cause, I was like, I'm
very intimately sort of

00:45:41.841 --> 00:45:44.064
like thinking about all this
during the writing process,

00:45:44.064 --> 00:45:47.726
and then I kind of want
to separate myself from it

00:45:47.726 --> 00:45:51.446
so I can be as much of an
audience member when I watch it.

00:45:51.446 --> 00:45:52.700
But that was really hard to do.

00:45:52.700 --> 00:45:54.836
I mean, when I watched the first cut,

00:45:54.836 --> 00:45:59.169
there were moments where I
began to feel the sort of

00:46:00.255 --> 00:46:03.494
frightened emotion that I
knew we were trying to go for.

00:46:03.494 --> 00:46:04.327
Mhm.

00:46:04.327 --> 00:46:05.230
But it's, it's just gonna be

00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:06.975
so much different for somebody
who doesn't know what's,

00:46:06.975 --> 00:46:08.231
what's going to happen.

00:46:08.231 --> 00:46:09.064
Yeah.

00:46:09.064 --> 00:46:11.151
Uh, and so that I'm
interested in that because.

00:46:11.151 --> 00:46:12.500
That's the beauty of the conceit.

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:15.482
Is that it, we've set the stage for you

00:46:15.482 --> 00:46:17.395
to be the most freaked out,

00:46:17.395 --> 00:46:20.702
because it's not at all
what you're expecting.

00:46:20.702 --> 00:46:22.681
It's kind of like when
I took Christy to see

00:46:22.681 --> 00:46:24.688
Get Out and like, right
before the movie started,

00:46:24.688 --> 00:46:26.696
she leaned over and she
was like, "Is this a,

00:46:26.696 --> 00:46:28.575
is this movie scary? Or...

00:46:29.899 --> 00:46:31.319
"Is it, is this a drunk?"

00:46:31.319 --> 00:46:34.115
That's like, that's the
perfect way to go into a movie.

00:46:34.115 --> 00:46:35.049
I was like, "I don't,

00:46:35.049 --> 00:46:37.990
I don't think you should know.
You should just watch it."

00:46:37.990 --> 00:46:39.364
Yeah. You didn't want her to,

00:46:39.364 --> 00:46:40.823
yeah, get scared preemptively.

00:46:40.823 --> 00:46:43.419
And, interesting dynamic.

00:46:43.419 --> 00:46:44.975
You know, we, when we
were filming this thing,

00:46:44.975 --> 00:46:47.232
like you said, we filmed over two nights.

00:46:47.232 --> 00:46:50.643
The, the first night
we filmed in the woods.

00:46:50.643 --> 00:46:51.533
And then the second night,

00:46:51.533 --> 00:46:55.627
we filmed in the creative house and, uh...

00:46:55.627 --> 00:46:58.460
We, we never do night shoots even,

00:46:59.474 --> 00:47:03.219
I think on Buddy System, season one,

00:47:03.219 --> 00:47:04.052
we did...

00:47:04.052 --> 00:47:04.954
We did a night shoot.

00:47:04.954 --> 00:47:05.825
We did one night shoot...

00:47:05.825 --> 00:47:06.658
Maybe a couple.

00:47:06.658 --> 00:47:08.293
Maybe, maybe a couple.

00:47:08.293 --> 00:47:11.192
And, um, it's difficult for us. Like,

00:47:11.192 --> 00:47:14.518
I was kinda nervous to like,
really like, okay, you know...

00:47:14.518 --> 00:47:15.980
Well, your bedtimes 9:30.

00:47:15.980 --> 00:47:17.384
The whole, the whole red bull

00:47:17.384 --> 00:47:19.532
and that whole scene at the beginning.

00:47:19.532 --> 00:47:21.182
We were really drinking the energy drink.

00:47:21.182 --> 00:47:22.111
It's true.

00:47:22.111 --> 00:47:22.969
I was drinking the hippo piss.

00:47:22.969 --> 00:47:25.069
Or hippo, whatever it was.

00:47:25.069 --> 00:47:28.273
Um, but you get into you're
out of there in the woods.

00:47:28.273 --> 00:47:30.407
And, uh, it's hard to shoot at night

00:47:30.407 --> 00:47:31.707
in the woods when you're like,

00:47:31.707 --> 00:47:33.968
having to trudge a bunch of stuff,

00:47:33.968 --> 00:47:36.169
um, a quarter of a mile down there.

00:47:36.169 --> 00:47:38.704
Of course, the team did
most, most of the trudging,

00:47:38.704 --> 00:47:39.954
I'll be honest.

00:47:41.669 --> 00:47:43.785
It was, it was a lot
of fun to be out there

00:47:43.785 --> 00:47:46.059
in the woods at night,
and to take a doorframe

00:47:46.059 --> 00:47:46.930
and set that up.

00:47:46.930 --> 00:47:50.430
Like, literally, that doorframe was there.

00:47:51.637 --> 00:47:54.619
We didn't computer generate anything.

00:47:54.619 --> 00:47:58.578
Most of the magic in the
cuts happened in swish pans.

00:47:58.578 --> 00:47:59.411
Yeah.

00:47:59.411 --> 00:48:01.545
But the biggest point of where

00:48:01.545 --> 00:48:03.496
you walk down the hallway and go through

00:48:03.496 --> 00:48:06.797
that middle doorway, and when you open it,

00:48:06.797 --> 00:48:10.184
it's not the recording room
like we showed earlier,

00:48:10.184 --> 00:48:12.684
but it's the, it's the forest.

00:48:14.365 --> 00:48:16.020
Ben felt. And we agreed that, like,

00:48:16.020 --> 00:48:17.939
we want to take a door
out there and we want

00:48:17.939 --> 00:48:18.772
to do this practically.

00:48:18.772 --> 00:48:20.097
And we don't want to, you know,

00:48:20.097 --> 00:48:22.200
we frame the door close enough

00:48:22.200 --> 00:48:23.594
so that it's already in the woods,

00:48:23.594 --> 00:48:26.652
but you don't know it, because
of the way that he's lit it.

00:48:26.652 --> 00:48:29.735
You open it up and then
there's the forest floor.

00:48:29.735 --> 00:48:31.399
And it's all real.

00:48:31.399 --> 00:48:32.232
Yeah.

00:48:32.232 --> 00:48:33.065
Like, there's no,

00:48:33.065 --> 00:48:37.993
there was no chicanery in post
to make that happen cause,

00:48:37.993 --> 00:48:40.411
we built, like Paisley came out there

00:48:40.411 --> 00:48:42.717
and they built the door frame.

00:48:42.717 --> 00:48:44.576
Yeah, which, quick note about that.

00:48:44.576 --> 00:48:47.565
Like, uh, everybody who
worked on this thing,

00:48:47.565 --> 00:48:49.560
with the exception, exception of Paige,

00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:52.384
was just, you know, a team, team member

00:48:52.384 --> 00:48:55.060
here at mythical that was kinda stepping

00:48:55.060 --> 00:48:57.395
into something that they don't always do.

00:48:57.395 --> 00:49:01.276
I mean, Jenna was involved
on the production side,

00:49:01.276 --> 00:49:02.859
Paisley built that,

00:49:05.329 --> 00:49:06.370
built that door.

00:49:06.370 --> 00:49:08.387
Which, she's always building
stuff for GMM, but it was...

00:49:08.387 --> 00:49:09.220
Yeah.

00:49:09.220 --> 00:49:10.994
And then of course, Ben
is kind of taking time

00:49:10.994 --> 00:49:13.450
out of what he was doing
on Mythical Kitchen.

00:49:13.450 --> 00:49:15.180
So, I think it was fun for us all

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:18.684
to kind of like get to do
something. I mean, it's fun.

00:49:18.684 --> 00:49:19.517
Oh, yeah.

00:49:19.517 --> 00:49:20.350
It's always fun for us to be like, hey,

00:49:20.350 --> 00:49:21.725
this is something that's different.

00:49:21.725 --> 00:49:24.021
And it, it like, it's,
it's not this giant thing

00:49:24.021 --> 00:49:25.537
that we're throwing
ourselves in for, into,

00:49:25.537 --> 00:49:28.819
for months and completely
