We shot everything else.

00:50:45.610 --> 00:50:47.035
Yeah. We shot past sunup.

00:50:47.035 --> 00:50:47.868
That was literally,

00:50:47.868 --> 00:50:48.701
Yeah. We actually,

00:50:48.701 --> 00:50:51.409
the last couple of shots,
we had to make sure

00:50:51.409 --> 00:50:53.247
that the windows were not letting light in

00:50:53.247 --> 00:50:56.461
because we were getting into the daylight.

00:50:56.461 --> 00:51:01.461
Uh, you know, Stevie also
being there was very helpful.

00:51:01.616 --> 00:51:04.005
I remember one instance
when we're shooting

00:51:04.005 --> 00:51:05.007
at the, the creative house,

00:51:05.007 --> 00:51:08.193
the first few scenes of like setting up

00:51:08.193 --> 00:51:11.530
the farce of it being an interactive song,

00:51:11.530 --> 00:51:13.576
writing thing, like the,
the first few scenes

00:51:13.576 --> 00:51:17.235
where we're like supposed
to be totally ourselves.

00:51:17.235 --> 00:51:20.381
And we don't want to give
any inkling that we're,

00:51:20.381 --> 00:51:22.368
we're acting like the actor
versions of ourselves.

00:51:22.368 --> 00:51:23.201
Mhm.

00:51:23.201 --> 00:51:25.866
There's this weird thing
that happens that we,

00:51:25.866 --> 00:51:29.660
we had to face, which was, we've got to,

00:51:29.660 --> 00:51:31.283
we've got to be ourselves.

00:51:31.283 --> 00:51:35.773
I mean, we've gotta be like
really in like vlog GMM mode.

00:51:35.773 --> 00:51:39.207
And at some point, we're going
to be going off of a script

00:51:39.207 --> 00:51:41.810
and we're going to be
making that transition

00:51:41.810 --> 00:51:44.919
to still acting like
ourselves. But in this...

00:51:44.919 --> 00:51:45.993
But now we're really acting.

00:51:45.993 --> 00:51:47.868
We're doing something that
wouldn't happen to us.

00:51:47.868 --> 00:51:48.841
Yeah, in this real word.

00:51:48.841 --> 00:51:50.295
We're reacting to things
that wouldn't happen to us.

00:51:50.295 --> 00:51:52.378
So, it's great to have,

00:51:53.478 --> 00:51:57.593
like Stevie and Ben there, who are like,

00:51:57.593 --> 00:52:00.010
"You need to play this scene,

00:52:01.258 --> 00:52:03.639
like Rhett. You would respond to Lincoln

00:52:03.639 --> 00:52:06.278
this way behind the desk at GMM.

00:52:06.278 --> 00:52:07.947
And I don't feel like you're doing that."

00:52:07.947 --> 00:52:11.539
Or, you have given me similar
direction where it was.

00:52:11.539 --> 00:52:14.834
And I think that's a big test is if,

00:52:14.834 --> 00:52:17.219
if people watch the first
few clips and they're like,

00:52:17.219 --> 00:52:18.983
"Why are they acting weird?"

00:52:18.983 --> 00:52:20.337
Then, we're a bit stuck.

00:52:20.337 --> 00:52:22.503
I'm, I'm pretty confident
that, that I'm my main...

00:52:22.503 --> 00:52:23.336
I feel good about it.

00:52:23.336 --> 00:52:25.121
My main concern is that they're just,

00:52:25.121 --> 00:52:26.267
most people are going to be like,

00:52:26.267 --> 00:52:28.585
"I don't really care
about you guys bringing

00:52:28.585 --> 00:52:30.271
me along the ride to write a song."

00:52:30.271 --> 00:52:33.271
But I'm saying that from a
performance standpoint...

00:52:33.271 --> 00:52:34.104
Yeah.

00:52:34.104 --> 00:52:38.325
It was, it was a fun challenge
to think of it that way.

00:52:38.325 --> 00:52:41.935
That we're making this
performance transition

00:52:41.935 --> 00:52:45.812
from completely, or very loosely scripted,

00:52:45.812 --> 00:52:47.901
to pretty tightly scripted.

00:52:47.901 --> 00:52:48.859
Yeah, yeah.

00:52:48.859 --> 00:52:53.692
So every aspect of it was, was
challenging in its own way.

00:52:54.926 --> 00:52:57.445
That was part of why we
wanted to do it, you know,

00:52:57.445 --> 00:52:59.227
just to like scratch those itches

00:52:59.227 --> 00:53:02.424
and, uh, get the creative juices flowing.

00:53:02.424 --> 00:53:04.569
And, we do think, I mean, I'll, I'll,

00:53:04.569 --> 00:53:07.778
I'll say that I also have a secret hope

00:53:07.778 --> 00:53:11.588
that, um, in the way that some of these

00:53:11.588 --> 00:53:14.422
Twitter threads and whatever,

00:53:14.422 --> 00:53:15.255
they might be.

00:53:15.255 --> 00:53:16.905
Things that start kind of small on,

00:53:16.905 --> 00:53:18.209
on the internet.

00:53:18.209 --> 00:53:20.027
Like there is a world here to explore,

00:53:20.027 --> 00:53:22.718
not necessarily, the exact
thing that we explored...

00:53:22.718 --> 00:53:23.551
Mhm.

00:53:23.551 --> 00:53:25.847
But the concept of the album

00:53:25.847 --> 00:53:27.342
and being trapped in an album.

00:53:27.342 --> 00:53:28.647
And it's been there forever.

00:53:28.647 --> 00:53:30.143
Like, what's the origin story of that?

00:53:30.143 --> 00:53:31.051
Like I would love,

00:53:31.051 --> 00:53:33.182
I would love to be able
to tell that story,

00:53:33.182 --> 00:53:35.573
not necessarily as a buddy
comedy with the two of us.

00:53:35.573 --> 00:53:38.771
Who knows, but, so I
really hope people love it.

00:53:38.771 --> 00:53:41.749
Now, one thing that
happened that just speak

00:53:41.749 --> 00:53:44.832
to the nature of doing this on TikTok

00:53:46.250 --> 00:53:49.274
and a platform that we're
learning is, you know,

00:53:49.274 --> 00:53:51.508
we had this idea for this scene,

00:53:51.508 --> 00:53:55.212
with the crow, where the
crow hits the, the door,

00:53:55.212 --> 00:53:58.824
and then we go outside and
we see it and it's dead.

00:53:58.824 --> 00:53:59.993
And there's a moment that we have

00:53:59.993 --> 00:54:01.627
where that, it ends
with me throwing it away

00:54:01.627 --> 00:54:02.966
and Link accusing me of littering.

00:54:02.966 --> 00:54:06.475
And then, by the way, if you
look at the album closely,

00:54:06.475 --> 00:54:08.282
like rewatch it and look at the album.

00:54:08.282 --> 00:54:09.834
The album. Anytime the album is shown,

00:54:09.834 --> 00:54:11.188
it's very intentional and yeah,

00:54:11.188 --> 00:54:13.963
it will change, maybe in ways
that you did not anticipate

00:54:13.963 --> 00:54:16.422
or see the first time.
But we were out there.

00:54:16.422 --> 00:54:18.999
We had this, we had this replica crow.

00:54:18.999 --> 00:54:20.135
It was, and we're out
there, gettin' ready to...

00:54:20.135 --> 00:54:22.135
It seemed like it was
the weight of a crow.

00:54:22.135 --> 00:54:22.968
Yeah.

00:54:22.968 --> 00:54:23.801
It was very good.

00:54:23.801 --> 00:54:24.634
And I'm out there,

00:54:24.634 --> 00:54:27.121
and, so, putting this towel over it,

00:54:27.121 --> 00:54:30.056
and that's when Greg, who was
doing the behind the scenes,

00:54:30.056 --> 00:54:32.734
and also works on our social media

00:54:32.734 --> 00:54:35.799
and knows more about TikTok than we do,

00:54:35.799 --> 00:54:37.892
was he just kind of says in the moment,

00:54:37.892 --> 00:54:40.284
he's like, "Yeah, I hope that,

00:54:40.284 --> 00:54:45.043
that I hope that this part
of it is not misinterpreted

00:54:45.043 --> 00:54:47.467
because, sometimes on
TikTok, if things are,

00:54:47.467 --> 00:54:49.473
if people think that you're
doing something with the,

00:54:49.473 --> 00:54:51.709
you know, it's conceivable
that if people thought

00:54:51.709 --> 00:54:54.385
you were doing something
with actual dead animal,

00:54:54.385 --> 00:54:56.499
that people would get upset,

00:54:56.499 --> 00:54:58.664
and they could pull that video down."

00:54:58.664 --> 00:55:03.664
Yeah. Is Rhett really throwing
a dead crow in the woods?

00:55:03.802 --> 00:55:05.982
There was actually a, there was a scene,

00:55:05.982 --> 00:55:08.982
so at that point we were like,
okay, well, what is scripted?

00:55:08.982 --> 00:55:10.694
What you've planned to do.

00:55:10.694 --> 00:55:13.774
And even when we shot test
footage of was you picking up

00:55:13.774 --> 00:55:16.094
the crow and you making
fun of me for saying,

00:55:16.094 --> 00:55:18.501
"I think it will come to
just give it a minute,"

00:55:18.501 --> 00:55:20.222
and you pick it up and
you're, and I'm like,

00:55:20.222 --> 00:55:21.055
"What are you doing?" You're like,

00:55:21.055 --> 00:55:23.318
"Well, I'm going to give it a toss and see

00:55:23.318 --> 00:55:24.302
if it'll..."

00:55:24.302 --> 00:55:25.300
Give it a little headstart.

00:55:25.300 --> 00:55:26.497
"Give it a little headstart on flying."

00:55:26.497 --> 00:55:28.321
Give it, give it, a jumpstart,
like a paper airplane.

00:55:28.321 --> 00:55:32.867
And you throw it. And it just
plops down on the concrete.

00:55:32.867 --> 00:55:33.700
And it's hilarious.

00:55:33.700 --> 00:55:35.234
Hilarious, very funny.

00:55:35.234 --> 00:55:36.811
And then you pick it up again,

00:55:36.811 --> 00:55:38.816
and that's when we talk
about the rotisserie chicken,

00:55:38.816 --> 00:55:40.252
and you, you throw it in there.

00:55:40.252 --> 00:55:42.535
You say test footage. I mean,
we shot that as a scene.

00:55:42.535 --> 00:55:43.691
We have that intact,

00:55:43.691 --> 00:55:46.378
which we are probably
showing to you right now.

00:55:46.378 --> 00:55:48.810
What are you going to do with it?

00:55:48.810 --> 00:55:49.643
I don't know.

00:55:49.643 --> 00:55:52.238
Maybe it'll come back to, if
I give him a little jumpstart

00:55:52.238 --> 00:55:53.638
like a paper airplane.

00:55:54.599 --> 00:55:55.766
Nope.

00:55:57.482 --> 00:56:00.309
Dude... He's fricking dead, man.

00:56:00.309 --> 00:56:01.261
Okay.

00:56:01.261 --> 00:56:02.832
I'm throwing him in the bushes.

00:56:02.832 --> 00:56:05.216
Wh, what? Hold on,
wouldn't that be littering?

00:56:05.216 --> 00:56:08.323
We're having a debate in the
moment of, is that too much?

00:56:08.323 --> 00:56:10.879
Is throwing the crow and
having it plopped down people,

00:56:10.879 --> 00:56:13.521
the risk of people
thinking it's a real crow

00:56:13.521 --> 00:56:17.019
and getting upset, TikTok might
be flagging it, and, like,

00:56:17.019 --> 00:56:20.949
if you remove this, this one
clip from the whole thing,

00:56:20.949 --> 00:56:22.594
is that thing confusing and frustrating?

00:56:22.594 --> 00:56:23.638
Yes, yes.

00:56:23.638 --> 00:56:24.658
And it's like,

00:56:24.658 --> 00:56:26.154
so we, you know, we're like, well,

00:56:26.154 --> 00:56:29.537
let's just shoot another
version of this particular scene

00:56:29.537 --> 00:56:31.192
where you don't plop it on the ground.

00:56:31.192 --> 00:56:32.025
You just throw it in the woods

00:56:32.025 --> 00:56:35.684
and maybe that'll mitigate
it, and we'll decide later.

00:56:35.684 --> 00:56:37.556
So, but yeah, because
it's so experimental,

00:56:37.556 --> 00:56:38.989
you get in your own head of like,

00:56:38.989 --> 00:56:40.467
what is the thing that we're forgetting,

00:56:40.467 --> 00:56:42.781
that's going to bring
this whole thing down?

00:56:42.781 --> 00:56:44.608
And I remember, in that moment,
it was a bit of a crisis.

00:56:44.608 --> 00:56:48.684
If that becomes the reason
that this doesn't work,

00:56:48.684 --> 00:56:49.748
That sucks.

00:56:49.748 --> 00:56:50.581
And so, yeah, so we,

00:56:50.581 --> 00:56:54.331
so the toned down version
is in, is in there.

00:56:55.928 --> 00:56:57.807
So, we're not concerned
about it at this point.

00:56:57.807 --> 00:57:00.569
It's not, again, it's not as funny.

00:57:00.569 --> 00:57:02.494
It's not as funny as me
throwing the crow up in the air

00:57:02.494 --> 00:57:03.327
and having a hit the ground.

00:57:03.327 --> 00:57:05.882
But again, for, that, that could be...

00:57:05.882 --> 00:57:06.909
It's dead animal humor.

00:57:06.909 --> 00:57:09.415
Maybe people don't like
that anyway, it wasn't,

00:57:09.415 --> 00:57:11.507
it wasn't necessary.

00:57:11.507 --> 00:57:14.155
So, you know, it's a matter of taste.

00:57:14.155 --> 00:57:14.988
Yeah.

00:57:14.988 --> 00:57:17.190
If not a matter of, uh,

00:57:17.190 --> 00:57:20.019
like content restrictions and flagging.

00:57:20.019 --> 00:57:24.938
Which we, got a reasonable
certainty that it wasn't.

00:57:24.938 --> 00:57:28.388
But let's, let's skip to TikTok at large.

00:57:28.388 --> 00:57:33.388
I think that, this is obviously
a strategy, but in parallel,

00:57:34.350 --> 00:57:38.367
because we've been developing
Hazel for many months,

00:57:38.367 --> 00:57:39.651
we're also trying to figure out,

00:57:39.651 --> 00:57:44.068
what's our general TikTok
strategy because we're the,

00:57:45.013 --> 00:57:47.487
the audience is over there is undeniable.

00:57:47.487 --> 00:57:48.320
Yeah.

00:57:48.320 --> 00:57:50.331
And so we made a decision.

00:57:50.331 --> 00:57:51.537
Was it pre-pandemic?

00:57:51.537 --> 00:57:52.966
Yeah. Cause we were in the boardroom.

00:57:52.966 --> 00:57:53.799
I remember...

00:57:53.799 --> 00:57:54.840
That's right.

00:57:54.840 --> 00:57:56.021
I remember the moment. We
were all sitting together.

00:57:56.021 --> 00:57:57.371
Are we gonna do TikTok?

00:57:57.371 --> 00:58:00.055
Is Mythical going to be, are
we really going to try this?

00:58:00.055 --> 00:58:02.255
Are we gonna, are we going
to allocate resources

00:58:02.255 --> 00:58:03.116
to this when there's...

00:58:03.116 --> 00:58:03.949
There's no money in it.

00:58:03.949 --> 00:58:06.510
There's no direct revenue sharing?

00:58:06.510 --> 00:58:07.343
Yeah.

00:58:07.343 --> 00:58:08.661
Uh, so it's hard to justify from

00:58:08.661 --> 00:58:10.734
a financial, business standpoint.

00:58:10.734 --> 00:58:14.184
But from a strategic
standpoint, we said this,

00:58:14.184 --> 00:58:17.319
I mean, this is not a flash in the pan.

00:58:17.319 --> 00:58:18.152
This is happening.

00:58:18.152 --> 00:58:21.508
Well, I specifically
remember thinking and saying,

00:58:21.508 --> 00:58:24.788
I think, in that moment when
we were considering it, like,

00:58:24.788 --> 00:58:27.256
I don't want this to be,

00:58:27.256 --> 00:58:29.756
there are these moments in the birth

00:58:29.756 --> 00:58:32.979
of social media platforms
in which your ability

00:58:32.979 --> 00:58:35.706
to get traction like
right in the beginning,

00:58:35.706 --> 00:58:38.612
like you remember all those
people like Ashton Kutcher,

00:58:38.612 --> 00:58:40.581
how does he have so
many Twitter followers?

00:58:40.581 --> 00:58:41.414
Huh.

00:58:41.414 --> 00:58:43.164
Well, he got in and he took it seriously

00:58:43.164 --> 00:58:44.912
from the beginning.

00:58:44.912 --> 00:58:45.782
Now, if you're just like,

00:58:45.782 --> 00:58:46.837
I think I'm going to get on Twitter,

00:58:46.837 --> 00:58:51.145
like, getting a Twitter follower
these days and keeping it,

00:58:51.145 --> 00:58:53.590
as someone who's been pretty much

00:58:53.590 --> 00:58:56.061
the same number for a really,
really long time it's so hard.

00:58:56.061 --> 00:58:59.504
This, at this stage in the platform.

00:58:59.504 --> 00:59:01.257
But I was like, if we get in now,

00:59:01.257 --> 00:59:02.557
even if the only thing we're doing,

00:59:02.557 --> 00:59:05.410
because I think we said is,
let's just get on there.

00:59:05.410 --> 00:59:07.494
And let's just put some
GMM clips on there.

00:59:07.494 --> 00:59:10.208
Like, let's just have a
presence because getting

00:59:10.208 --> 00:59:12.312
on there and letting people
know that you're there.

00:59:12.312 --> 00:59:14.487
Even if you're not yet
doing anything strategic,

00:59:14.487 --> 00:59:16.817
you're going to get
followers. And that worked.

00:59:16.817 --> 00:59:19.299
And we got to a million
followers pretty quickly

00:59:19.299 --> 00:59:20.261
and it was like, okay, good.

00:59:20.261 --> 00:59:22.222
All right, we're, we a foothold here

00:59:22.222 --> 00:59:24.677
on this thing that seems like
it's going to be important

00:59:24.677 --> 00:59:27.021
in the, the larger sort of conversation.

00:59:27.021 --> 00:59:28.193
But then it was like,

00:59:28.193 --> 00:59:31.212
what are we actually going to do?

00:59:31.212 --> 00:59:33.338
And me and you, in the
beginning of the pandemic,

00:59:33.338 --> 00:59:35.209
cause I remember I was talking about this

00:59:35.209 --> 00:59:37.292
uh, when we were doing our vlogs.

00:59:37.292 --> 00:59:41.781
We actually started to
record for a potential vlog

00:59:41.781 --> 00:59:44.940
on the Rhett and Link channel,
when we were doing those,

00:59:44.940 --> 00:59:46.645
the process that we were coming up with,

00:59:46.645 --> 00:59:48.098
because a lot of people were like,

00:59:48.098 --> 00:59:50.458
what is happening on the Mythical TikTok?

00:59:50.458 --> 00:59:52.017
Like, what are Rhett and Link doing?

00:59:52.017 --> 00:59:53.808
Who's writing these TikToks?

00:59:53.808 --> 00:59:54.641
Yeah.

00:59:54.641 --> 00:59:57.232
So, we wanted to do a Rhett and Link vlog

00:59:57.232 --> 01:00:02.062
where we talked about our
experimentation on TikTok

01:00:02.062 --> 01:00:05.444
in documenting that
process. But then it became,

01:00:05.444 --> 01:00:07.980
it became a lot to
figure out what we wanted

01:00:07.980 --> 01:00:09.842
to try on TikTok personally.

01:00:09.842 --> 01:00:12.492
And then to have to document it ourselves.

01:00:12.492 --> 01:00:14.261
It just became a bit much. So.

01:00:14.261 --> 01:00:15.744
But the thing that we have been doing,

01:00:15.744 --> 01:00:17.679
that you may have noticed if you like,

01:00:17.679 --> 01:00:19.831
if you've been watching
us for a really long time,

01:00:19.831 --> 01:00:22.188
you may have picked up on this now. Yes,

01:00:22.188 --> 01:00:25.740
there are a lot of things that
we sort of do as a company

01:00:25.740 --> 01:00:28.215
that Link and I lend ourselves to

01:00:28.215 --> 01:00:30.281
and kind of throw
ourselves into and you can,

01:00:30.281 --> 01:00:31.526
and you might be like,

01:00:31.526 --> 01:00:32.851
"I don't know if that's something

01:00:32.851 --> 01:00:34.348
that Rhett and Link
would have come up with,

01:00:34.348 --> 01:00:37.174
but I can see why they
did it," or, or whatever.

01:00:37.174 --> 01:00:41.839
We threw ourselves into
TikTok on a very personal

01:00:41.839 --> 01:00:44.246
like, level, and kind of went back to

01:00:44.246 --> 01:00:49.038
that initial mentality of
the early days of YouTube

01:00:49.038 --> 01:00:50.203
in which we were creators,

01:00:50.203 --> 01:00:52.106
trying to figure out a new platform,

01:00:52.106 --> 01:00:53.889
throwing things out there, experimenting,

01:00:53.889 --> 01:00:56.651
throwing a lot of things at
the wall, getting reaction.

01:00:56.651 --> 01:00:58.542
Now, that's not a full-time thing for us,

01:00:58.542 --> 01:01:00.414
because of all the other stuff that we do.

01:01:00.414 --> 01:01:03.185
But when we engaged with it
over the past year and a half or

01:01:03.185 --> 01:01:05.727
so, that's how we would do it.

01:01:05.727 --> 01:01:07.355
We would like, let's learn all we can.

01:01:07.355 --> 01:01:10.524
And then the, the team
was also developing things

01:01:10.524 --> 01:01:13.586
that we could, that Mythical
could try on our TikTok.

01:01:13.586 --> 01:01:16.777
But what we were doing at
the creative house was like,

01:01:16.777 --> 01:01:20.412
we were getting down on all
fours and filming each other...

01:01:20.412 --> 01:01:21.294
As dogs.

01:01:21.294 --> 01:01:25.819
As, as dogs or as these,
like strange superheroes

01:01:25.819 --> 01:01:27.843
having a video conference.

01:01:27.843 --> 01:01:29.486
Yeah, we were just trying
some character based stuff.

01:01:29.486 --> 01:01:30.651
Trying things.

01:01:30.651 --> 01:01:33.442
Uh, I can't remember what
else we tried, but then we...

01:01:33.442 --> 01:01:35.528
Also just some straight up sketch stuff.

01:01:35.528 --> 01:01:38.161
And a lot of this still is
on, some of it was taken down

01:01:38.161 --> 01:01:39.107
because it just didn't do well.

01:01:39.107 --> 01:01:41.566
A lot of it is still on
there from over a year ago.

01:01:41.566 --> 01:01:42.912
That kind of fizzled out.

01:01:42.912 --> 01:01:45.884
I think we got distracted
or pulled into other things,

01:01:45.884 --> 01:01:48.788
and it didn't take off.
So it got a little,

01:01:48.788 --> 01:01:49.621
it didn't really work.

01:01:49.621 --> 01:01:50.454
It got disheartening.

01:01:50.454 --> 01:01:51.287
Yeah.

01:01:51.287 --> 01:01:53.490
And there were other
things that were working.

01:01:53.490 --> 01:01:57.865
And then when we came back
to it, many months later,

01:01:57.865 --> 01:02:01.782
we started doing some,
some sketch based stuff,

01:02:03.193 --> 01:02:06.275
but we wanted to hone, and we're still

01:02:06.275 --> 01:02:10.275
in the process of honing
what Mythical sketches,

01:02:11.158 --> 01:02:15.854
what Rhett and Link sketches
on TikTok look and feel like.

01:02:15.854 --> 01:02:18.725
And, so we're like, I would
want them to be conversational.

01:02:18.725 --> 01:02:21.932
We don't him to, you know,
we don't, we, we learned,

01:02:21.932 --> 01:02:24.940
we didn't want to go to prop
heavy or we didn't want to,

01:02:24.940 --> 01:02:27.413
we wanted to be able to turn
these things out quickly.

01:02:27.413 --> 01:02:29.994
We have to be super the engineer in us,

01:02:29.994 --> 01:02:32.210
but also just the very busy people in us.

01:02:32.210 --> 01:02:34.306
We have to be super efficient
with everything we do.

01:02:34.306 --> 01:02:36.881
So, we really need to be able to shoot

01:02:36.881 --> 01:02:39.858
one of these TikTok sketches in about,

01:02:39.858 --> 01:02:41.566
you know, 15 or 20 minutes.

01:02:41.566 --> 01:02:44.481
But it's shocking how
you can upload something

01:02:44.481 --> 01:02:46.516
and it will get like thousands of views

01:02:46.516 --> 01:02:48.315
and then you can upload something else

01:02:48.315 --> 01:02:51.589
and it can get millions. If
not, tens of millions of views.

01:02:51.589 --> 01:02:53.217
And they can be right next to each other

01:02:53.217 --> 01:02:55.192
and no influence on one another.

01:02:55.192 --> 01:02:57.955
So we're doing, right
now, we're doing sketches,

01:02:57.955 --> 01:03:00.704
we're doing trends, TikTok trends.

01:03:00.704 --> 01:03:03.204
And we're doing like GMM clips

01:03:04.069 --> 01:03:05.986
that can be TikTokized.

01:03:07.458 --> 01:03:08.291
Yeah.

01:03:08.291 --> 01:03:10.440
So that, where they actually
work for that platform.

01:03:10.440 --> 01:03:12.579
And don't feel just like an exported clip.

01:03:12.579 --> 01:03:15.071
And as we record this right now,

01:03:15.071 --> 01:03:17.983
like we've actually,
again, our team is great.

01:03:17.983 --> 01:03:19.296
I don't want to mischaracterize,

01:03:19.296 --> 01:03:21.448
when I say that we've
thrown ourselves into this.

01:03:21.448 --> 01:03:24.113
I'm just saying that like,
we've really gotten involved

01:03:24.113 --> 01:03:26.278
on the creative side,
but that doesn't mean

01:03:26.278 --> 01:03:27.192
we're doing it all.

01:03:27.192 --> 01:03:28.025
Our team is doing...

01:03:28.025 --> 01:03:28.858
Right.

01:03:28.858 --> 01:03:30.063
So much good work.

01:03:30.063 --> 01:03:33.026
And at this point the
creative process is like,

01:03:33.026 --> 01:03:37.165
we've got our internal
team pitching and writing.

01:03:37.165 --> 01:03:41.013
We've got, we actually have
some, uh, outside writers

01:03:41.013 --> 01:03:43.392
who make their own TikTok
that we like that are,

01:03:43.392 --> 01:03:45.475
that are pitching and writing sketches.

01:03:45.475 --> 01:03:48.062
We're pitching and writing
sketches ourselves.

01:03:48.062 --> 01:03:50.587
And, and it's just become this sort of,

01:03:50.587 --> 01:03:52.926
like old school collaborative.

01:03:52.926 --> 01:03:54.743
Let's see what people respond to.

01:03:54.743 --> 01:03:57.964
But, as of like right
now, as we record this,

01:03:57.964 --> 01:04:00.098
we're getting some reaction.

01:04:00.098 --> 01:04:02.255
Like we're, get, it feels like some people

01:04:02.255 --> 01:04:04.041
are literally saying
things like you call it.

01:04:04.041 --> 01:04:06.069
You guys are kind of finally getting

01:04:06.069 --> 01:04:07.457
the hang of this platform.

01:04:07.457 --> 01:04:09.348
When it was clear that
you were just kind of...

01:04:09.348 --> 01:04:10.181
Mhm.

01:04:10.181 --> 01:04:12.327
Trying a million different
things just a few months ago.

01:04:12.327 --> 01:04:14.349
It's cool to read that. I mean, it, it,

01:04:14.349 --> 01:04:16.826
it stings a little because it's like, hey,

01:04:16.826 --> 01:04:17.844
you're reading our mail.

01:04:17.844 --> 01:04:19.834
You know that like, but of course,

01:04:19.834 --> 01:04:21.608
yeah, we're trying to figure this out,

01:04:21.608 --> 01:04:25.439
but that's what we do as
individuals, as a duo,

01:04:25.439 --> 01:04:27.826
as a company. What we do is we say,

01:04:27.826 --> 01:04:30.468
"All right, what is the next thing?

01:04:30.468 --> 01:04:31.584
How do we figure it out?"

01:04:31.584 --> 01:04:36.229
And we gotta be willing to
get some egg on our face,

01:04:36.229 --> 01:04:37.531
by the way, write that down.

01:04:37.531 --> 01:04:38.364
I bet you there's something in that.

01:04:38.364 --> 01:04:40.077
Like, a literal egg on the face.

01:04:40.077 --> 01:04:41.627
Hah, that'll be hilarious.

01:04:41.627 --> 01:04:43.101
Right, right. Mhm.

01:04:43.101 --> 01:04:45.154
In order to learn something.

01:04:45.154 --> 01:04:48.746
And, um, but yeah, there
was lots of, I mean,

01:04:48.746 --> 01:04:53.459
the things that we would try
and when it doesn't work and

01:04:53.459 --> 01:04:54.292
you can't understand why,

01:04:54.292 --> 01:04:57.209
it's much easier to understand why,

01:04:58.089 --> 01:04:59.915
to figure out why something does work,

01:04:59.915 --> 01:05:01.960
than to figure out why
something didn't work.

01:05:01.960 --> 01:05:02.793
You know?

01:05:03.852 --> 01:05:04.752
And, this is a platform...

01:05:04.752 --> 01:05:06.353
Even if you're wrong about the thing,

01:05:06.353 --> 01:05:07.777
that why it worked,

01:05:07.777 --> 01:05:11.582
you feel really certain why
it worked because it just,

01:05:11.582 --> 01:05:14.020
the most plausible explanation.

01:05:14.020 --> 01:05:15.738
I don't know, it tends to fall into place,

01:05:15.738 --> 01:05:17.565
but then you're with the negative.

01:05:17.565 --> 01:05:20.418
It's much harder to pinpoint it.

01:05:20.418 --> 01:05:24.890
But it's so interesting
to be working in a,

01:05:24.890 --> 01:05:26.749
in a platform that you don't know.

01:05:26.749 --> 01:05:30.498
Like, we've learned
YouTube over the course of,

01:05:30.498 --> 01:05:31.477
the time being on there.

01:05:31.477 --> 01:05:34.308
So, we know in general, like, okay.

01:05:34.308 --> 01:05:36.826
You can pitch us an idea
for a YouTube video,

01:05:36.826 --> 01:05:39.799
and we can tell you, that's
probably not going to work.

01:05:39.799 --> 01:05:41.108
And here's why, or that has a chance,

01:05:41.108 --> 01:05:43.084
a good chance of working, and here's why.

01:05:43.084 --> 01:05:43.917
Mhm.

01:05:43.917 --> 01:05:48.288
The principles that go
into a TikTok video,

01:05:48.288 --> 01:05:51.371
you know, title and thumbnail are so,

01:05:53.292 --> 01:05:54.125
you know, strategic when it
comes to a YouTube video,

01:05:54.125 --> 01:05:54.958
Mhm.

01:05:56.214 --> 01:05:58.621
Because people are making a decision

01:05:58.621 --> 01:06:02.163
in an environment where their
decision for the next video

01:06:02.163 --> 01:06:05.497
is based exclusively
on title and thumbnail.

01:06:05.497 --> 01:06:07.674
That's not how TikTok works, right?

01:06:07.674 --> 01:06:10.424
TikTok works on people's reaction

01:06:11.616 --> 01:06:13.849
to the beginning of the video.

01:06:13.849 --> 01:06:16.744
Whatever happens in the
first 1, 2, 3 seconds

01:06:16.744 --> 01:06:19.260
of the video is how they
determine if they're going

01:06:19.260 --> 01:06:20.843
to keep watching that or swipe.

01:06:20.843 --> 01:06:21.690
And then of course,

01:06:21.690 --> 01:06:25.464
that data spread across
the bunches of people

01:06:25.464 --> 01:06:29.056
who are watching that,
tell something to TikTok,

01:06:29.056 --> 01:06:32.643
which then tells something
to, uh, the system and the,

01:06:32.643 --> 01:06:35.332
for you page, which
determines if that video

01:06:35.332 --> 01:06:36.830
is going to get shown to more people.

01:06:36.830 --> 01:06:38.976
And that's why you can have
a video that literally has

01:06:38.976 --> 01:06:40.309
11 million views

01:06:41.355 --> 01:06:43.791
next to one that's got
a 100 thousand views.

01:06:43.791 --> 01:06:44.624
On the same channel.

01:06:44.624 --> 01:06:46.011
Or 50 thousand views.

01:06:46.011 --> 01:06:46.844
Yeah.

01:06:46.844 --> 01:06:47.677
Right next to each other,

01:06:47.677 --> 01:06:49.543
which does not typically happen on YouTube

01:06:49.543 --> 01:06:50.786
because people's habits are different.

01:06:50.786 --> 01:06:53.615
It's a subconscious manipulation.

01:06:53.615 --> 01:06:57.216
You're, you're not, typically
when you're scrolling,

01:06:57.216 --> 01:06:59.951
you're not making an
active decision. It's this,

01:06:59.951 --> 01:07:03.441
it's a passive decision
that you're hooked.

01:07:03.441 --> 01:07:05.787
So you find yourself breaking your,

01:07:05.787 --> 01:07:09.151
your scroll because something
peaked your interest.

01:07:09.151 --> 01:07:12.401
It was something you read.
It was something that you,

01:07:12.401 --> 01:07:16.758
you heard that gripped
you, that was irresistible.

01:07:16.758 --> 01:07:21.673
Like you, um, and then is
that enough to keep you?

01:07:21.673 --> 01:07:23.953
Is the hook set deep enough? And are we,

01:07:23.953 --> 01:07:27.882
are we retaining your, your experience,

01:07:27.882 --> 01:07:31.143
keeping you from like,
oh no, this isn't what,

01:07:31.143 --> 01:07:33.607
this is not worthy of
me stopping scrolling.

01:07:33.607 --> 01:07:34.440
Imma keep going.

01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:38.357
All of that data is, is,
is scarily insightful.

01:07:40.738 --> 01:07:42.995
But the way that we have to interpret,

01:07:42.995 --> 01:07:46.275
it is just, did it work or
not? Did it halfway work?

01:07:46.275 --> 01:07:49.078
There's a lot of speculation
about the algorithm,

01:07:49.078 --> 01:07:51.262
but it, but it does.

01:07:51.262 --> 01:07:53.159
The more that it's about human behavior,

01:07:53.159 --> 01:07:54.564
the more that we feel like we can

01:07:54.564 --> 01:07:57.396
do something about it,
right? And that's, that's,

01:07:57.396 --> 01:07:59.760
that's what the algorithm
is trying to do too.

01:07:59.760 --> 01:08:02.400
Decipher, decipher human behavior.

01:08:02.400 --> 01:08:06.191
We're coming at it from,
from different angles.

01:08:06.191 --> 01:08:09.989
That's a, it's, that's a fun challenge,

01:08:09.989 --> 01:08:13.429
but when things don't work and
then they keep not working,

01:08:13.429 --> 01:08:16.221
and maybe you've pre-shot a whole of these

01:08:16.221 --> 01:08:18.747
that starts to get, uh, disheartening.

01:08:18.747 --> 01:08:19.667
And there's also...

01:08:19.667 --> 01:08:23.528
It's like, oh, we've made an
investment that now is it,

01:08:23.528 --> 01:08:25.950
do we know it's, like, the next five

01:08:25.950 --> 01:08:27.004
that we've already shot?

01:08:27.004 --> 01:08:28.707
Do we know that none of
those are going to work?

01:08:28.707 --> 01:08:32.347
Or is there a pivot we can do with text

01:08:32.347 --> 01:08:35.847
or with something else within the tool to,

01:08:36.976 --> 01:08:38.658
to make this still work?

01:08:38.658 --> 01:08:40.742
Or to prove our, to prove
our theory wrong now

01:08:40.742 --> 01:08:42.900
that like, we're going
down the wrong path.

01:08:42.900 --> 01:08:45.198
Well, and there's the
challenge of not wanting

01:08:45.198 --> 01:08:47.031
to fall into the trap,

01:08:48.139 --> 01:08:51.699
which is a very difficult thing
not to do when you've turned

01:08:51.699 --> 01:08:54.366
your creativity into a business.

01:08:55.273 --> 01:08:58.497
And that is, to just become
reactive and try to find,

01:08:58.497 --> 01:09:00.708
and then your only measure of success

01:09:00.708 --> 01:09:02.790
is if people are watching it right now...

01:09:02.790 --> 01:09:03.623
Right.

01:09:03.623 --> 01:09:04.654
People have to watch it in order for us

01:09:04.654 --> 01:09:06.753
to continue to feel like it's worth doing.

01:09:06.753 --> 01:09:08.406
Even though there isn't really any money,

01:09:08.406 --> 01:09:10.731
like occasionally it'll
be like a sponsorship deal

01:09:10.731 --> 01:09:13.497
or whatever, but there's
not a lot of money in it.

01:09:13.497 --> 01:09:16.192
Maybe there will be at
some point, but what that,

01:09:16.192 --> 01:09:19.246
what that means is, and I
think to come back to the,

01:09:19.246 --> 01:09:23.078
the Hazel thing for a second,
is it makes the Hazel project

01:09:23.078 --> 01:09:25.538
for me that much more special because,

01:09:25.538 --> 01:09:28.588
it really isn't a reaction to, oh,

01:09:28.588 --> 01:09:30.410
people are definitely
going to click on this.

01:09:30.410 --> 01:09:31.710
People are going to
care about this because

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:33.465
of what we've learned
about the way TikTok...

01:09:33.465 --> 01:09:34.298
No.

01:09:34.298 --> 01:09:35.802
Takes people's behavior into account.

01:09:35.802 --> 01:09:37.793
It's more like, hey,
here's a cool platform,

01:09:37.793 --> 01:09:42.327
that seems to be at a stage
where you can still experiment.

01:09:42.327 --> 01:09:43.160
And it's not just,

01:09:43.160 --> 01:09:45.374
you have to just do
this cookie cutter thing

01:09:45.374 --> 01:09:47.214
in order to succeed.

01:09:47.214 --> 01:09:49.372
Is this a place where we
can throw something up there

01:09:49.372 --> 01:09:52.396
that nobody else is
doing in exactly this way

01:09:52.396 --> 01:09:55.708
and see what happens? The who, again,

01:09:55.708 --> 01:09:58.541
the platform isn't really made for

01:10:00.360 --> 01:10:03.646
11 part series that
launch all in one night,

01:10:03.646 --> 01:10:07.674
but is there something there
in this environment where it

01:10:07.674 --> 01:10:10.948
can work to the degree where
it leads to something else

01:10:10.948 --> 01:10:12.939
within that platform?

01:10:12.939 --> 01:10:17.815
Could it be part of the platform,
even evolving and changing

01:10:17.815 --> 01:10:19.902
and growing in what it's capable of?

01:10:19.902 --> 01:10:22.293
Could it be that this
idea and this project

01:10:22.293 --> 01:10:25.705
and just our, uh, moving forward with it

01:10:25.705 --> 01:10:28.617
leads us to something else that
may or may not be on TikTok?

01:10:28.617 --> 01:10:29.575
Mhm.

01:10:29.575 --> 01:10:30.892
I don't know, but that's,

01:10:30.892 --> 01:10:32.840
that's why we do things like that,

01:10:32.840 --> 01:10:35.739
so that we, it, it, it's fun to do it,

01:10:35.739 --> 01:10:38.106
and it's fun in the moment
and we're proud of it

01:10:38.106 --> 01:10:40.006
and happy to do it,

01:10:40.006 --> 01:10:42.348
but maybe it, it leads to
something even more interesting.

01:10:42.348 --> 01:10:46.908
And when it comes to our
general TikTok strategy,

01:10:46.908 --> 01:10:48.522
at this point, if we,

01:10:48.522 --> 01:10:51.675
if we just wanted to do the
stuff that got the most views,

01:10:51.675 --> 01:10:53.905
we would just chase the trends.

01:10:53.905 --> 01:10:54.738
And... we,

01:10:56.196 --> 01:11:00.945
you know, we, Greg would just
be pitching those things.

01:11:00.945 --> 01:11:04.723
And every day we'd be like,
let's do the next thing,

01:11:04.723 --> 01:11:08.188
but, we want to do other things as well.

01:11:08.188 --> 01:11:11.105
Like, that's undeniably successful,

01:11:11.957 --> 01:11:15.957
and it, it's fun and it's
engaging and it works.

01:11:17.378 --> 01:11:19.814
And that, so that's
exciting and rewarding,

01:11:19.814 --> 01:11:24.135
but we also want to express
ourselves as comedians.

01:11:24.135 --> 01:11:27.885
And it is the place
where we can very quickly

01:11:30.112 --> 01:11:31.076
stay in scripted...

01:11:31.076 --> 01:11:31.909
Yeah.

01:11:31.909 --> 01:11:33.824
And work on, work on our,

01:11:33.824 --> 01:11:37.324
our character work and our acting and our,

01:11:38.311 --> 01:11:40.657
our comedic timing and the, you know,

01:11:40.657 --> 01:11:41.744
the, that craft...

01:11:41.744 --> 01:11:42.577
Yeah.

01:11:42.577 --> 01:11:45.931
And assert ourselves, uh,
on that front and say,

01:11:45.931 --> 01:11:50.598
okay, part of Mythical's
brand, is this style of comedy.

01:11:51.494 --> 01:11:53.861
Something that maybe we, you used,

01:11:53.861 --> 01:11:55.995
you didn't know we used to do,

01:11:55.995 --> 01:11:59.292
but here's a reminder or an education that

01:11:59.292 --> 01:12:01.140
we still have this type of, uh,

01:12:01.140 --> 01:12:02.758
comedic workup up our sleeves.

01:12:02.758 --> 01:12:05.531
And now there's a place for
us to do it strategically.

01:12:05.531 --> 01:12:06.759
I think that's, that's sort of the...

01:12:06.759 --> 01:12:08.206
But we have to make it work.

01:12:08.206 --> 01:12:09.481
Stars aligned in the moment.

01:12:09.481 --> 01:12:10.876
Though, and it didn't end there,

01:12:10.876 --> 01:12:12.462
the jury still out on...

01:12:12.462 --> 01:12:14.044
We don't know exactly what
we're going to land on.

01:12:14.044 --> 01:12:15.064
How the sketch is going to work, but.

01:12:15.064 --> 01:12:15.897
But we do know...

01:12:15.897 --> 01:12:17.083
We have gotten some that do work.

01:12:17.083 --> 01:12:20.888
But we do know, with a few,
would maybe a few exceptions,

01:12:20.888 --> 01:12:23.079
but no real big
exceptions, like, you know,

01:12:23.079 --> 01:12:24.682
we started the Mythical Bits channel,

01:12:24.682 --> 01:12:27.581
which is taking the short
form stuff that we're doing

01:12:27.581 --> 01:12:31.405
for TikTok and Instagram,
and putting it on YouTube.

01:12:31.405 --> 01:12:32.244
And, you know,

01:12:32.244 --> 01:12:34.408
thank you to those of you
who are subscribed to that

01:12:34.408 --> 01:12:36.799
and are enjoying the videos over there.

01:12:36.799 --> 01:12:40.799
But I think it's plain to
see that YouTube is not

01:12:40.799 --> 01:12:44.058
the place currently for
that type of content.

01:12:44.058 --> 01:12:44.891
Like, it's not.

01:12:44.891 --> 01:12:46.110
Or, maybe...

01:12:46.110 --> 01:12:46.943
Maybe it will become...

01:12:46.943 --> 01:12:48.612
Maybe, maybe it will be, I mean,

01:12:48.612 --> 01:12:50.819
with mythical kitchen,

01:12:50.819 --> 01:12:52.344
they were having similar conversations.

01:12:52.344 --> 01:12:53.305
And then all of a sudden,

01:12:53.305 --> 01:12:56.553
one of their shorts from months
earlier started to blow up.

01:12:56.553 --> 01:12:57.386
Yeah.

01:12:57.386 --> 01:13:00.460
I can't remember off the top
of my head, which one it was.

01:13:00.460 --> 01:13:01.549
Yeah, I don't either, but yeah,

01:13:01.549 --> 01:13:03.446
that's the whole short
strategy for YouTube.

01:13:03.446 --> 01:13:07.145
YouTube is like, okay,
listen, this genre of video,

01:13:07.145 --> 01:13:10.170
this vertical short video
is obviously working.

01:13:10.170 --> 01:13:11.949
It's what the kids are into.

01:13:11.949 --> 01:13:12.782
It's amazing...

01:13:12.782 --> 01:13:13.615
They want to, they want to,

01:13:13.615 --> 01:13:15.179
they want to capture
that audience as well.

01:13:15.179 --> 01:13:17.111
It's amazing how you can come,

01:13:17.111 --> 01:13:18.877
you can think you're
coming to a conclusion,

01:13:18.877 --> 01:13:21.921
this isn't working. Do
we need to scrap it?

01:13:21.921 --> 01:13:25.321
And then, when we were
talking specifically about

01:13:25.321 --> 01:13:27.988
our scripted sketches on TikTok,

01:13:29.000 --> 01:13:31.591
I mean, one one day, in the afternoon,

01:13:31.591 --> 01:13:33.724
we were having a conversation about, like,

01:13:33.724 --> 01:13:35.551
the amount of resources
we were putting into it,

01:13:35.551 --> 01:13:37.146
and the amount of our time,

01:13:37.146 --> 01:13:40.086
and the lackluster performance.

01:13:40.086 --> 01:13:42.508
And then, as we were having

01:13:42.508 --> 01:13:45.097
the business conversation
a few hours later,

01:13:45.097 --> 01:13:47.068
we look on TikTok and the latest sketch

01:13:47.068 --> 01:13:49.496
that we put up about, um,

01:13:49.496 --> 01:13:52.768
the favor economy and the hot sauce,

01:13:52.768 --> 01:13:54.685
like that one took off.

01:13:56.184 --> 01:13:59.210
And, it changed the, our
conversation entirely,

01:13:59.210 --> 01:14:01.018
because we were like, hold on,

01:14:01.018 --> 01:14:03.573
we made a little tweak to
the text at the beginning

01:14:03.573 --> 01:14:06.262
of this, and that, well,
maybe that unlocks it.

01:14:06.262 --> 01:14:07.328
Well, to be specific,

01:14:07.328 --> 01:14:11.328
We put text on-screen at the
beginning of the, the sketch.

01:14:11.328 --> 01:14:14.434
And we had not been doing
that in the previous ones.

01:14:14.434 --> 01:14:16.055
And I'm not saying that the sketch itself,

01:14:16.055 --> 01:14:18.249
it doesn't stand on its
own and isn't relatable,

01:14:18.249 --> 01:14:20.469
and that's why people like it, but,

01:14:20.469 --> 01:14:24.033
I bet every dollar I've
ever seen that the decision

01:14:24.033 --> 01:14:27.153
to put the text at the
beginning was the key

